VLAN with Trunk Mode

What is Trunking?

A trunk port carries traffic from multiple VLANs between network devices, such as two switches.


Trunking allows VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 traffic to travel over a single physical link between the switches.

Why Use Trunking?

* Carries multiple VLANs over one link
* Connects VLANs across multiple switches
* Reduces the need for separate physical links
* Supports scalable network design
* Uses 802.1Q VLAN tagging to identify VLAN traffic

Configuration

Create VLANs

Switch(config)# vlan 10
Switch(config-vlan)# name SALES
Switch(config-vlan)# exit

Switch(config)# vlan 20
Switch(config-vlan)# name IT
Switch(config-vlan)# exit

Configure Access Ports

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if)# switchport access vlan 10
Switch(config-if)# exit

Configure Trunk Port

Assuming Fa0/24 connects the two switches:

Switch(config)# interface fa0/24
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode trunk
Switch(config-if)# exit

Configure the same trunk on the other switch.

Verification

show vlan brief
show interfaces trunk

show interfaces trunk verifies whether the trunk is active and which VLANs are allowed on it.

Key Concepts

Access Port → Carries traffic for a single VLAN

Trunk Port → Carries traffic for multiple VLANs

802.1Q → VLAN tagging standard used on trunk links

VLAN → Logical Layer 2 network

Native VLAN → VLAN whose frames are sent untagged on an 802.1Q trunk

